Partial Solution: I start from `venue`, which contains the venue name: `FROM venue AS t2`.
I join `paper` on the shared venue identifier so I can count papers associated with each venue: `FROM venue AS t2 JOIN paper AS t1 ON t2.venueid  =  t1.venueid`.
I restrict the joined papers to those published in 2015: `FROM venue AS t2 JOIN paper AS t1 ON t2.venueid  =  t1.venueid WHERE t1.year  =  2015`.
I further restrict the rows to the venue named nature communications: `FROM venue AS t2 JOIN paper AS t1 ON t2.venueid  =  t1.venueid WHERE t1.year  =  2015 AND t2.venuename  =  "nature communications"`.
I count the qualifying paper identifiers and retain the target's DISTINCT result expression, completing the query: `SELECT DISTINCT COUNT ( t1.paperid ) FROM venue AS t2 JOIN paper AS t1 ON t2.venueid  =  t1.venueid WHERE t1.year  =  2015 AND t2.venuename  =  "nature communications";`.

